' Surplus value: class model
' Abstract determinations rise toward the concrete capitalist production of surplus value.
' Companion notes: notes/surplus-value.md — full stereotype legend: notes/stereotypes.md
'
' Stereotypes (a type may carry more than one; see notes/stereotypes.md)
'   transhistorical       — material conditions of human life; no specific social form
'   historically specific — exists only under a definite social form (commodity
'                           production or capitalism); not "recent", "bound to a
'                           social form". Opposite of transhistorical.
'   abstract det.         — simple, one-sided determination; never exists on its own.
'                           A different cut to the one above: e.g. Value and
'                           AbstractLabour are both abstract det. AND historically
'                           specific (they are one-sided AND do not exist in every
'                           society) — do not collapse the two cuts into one.
'   social relation       — not a physical property of things
'   personification       — individual as bearer of a class relation
'
' Colour rule: skinparam BackgroundColor picks the FIRST stereotype declared on a
' class. Where a class carries several, the declaration order below is deliberate
' (usually abstract det. or personification first) so the diagram's colour tracks
' the class's primary pedagogical role, not merely its most recently-added tag.

@startuml surplus-value-overview
title Surplus value — rising from the abstract to the concrete
caption Legend and full stereotype meanings: notes/stereotypes.md

hide circle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam ranksep 70
skinparam nodesep 40
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<transhistorical>> #E8F5E9
  BackgroundColor<<abstract det.>> #E3F2FD
  BackgroundColor<<social relation>> #EDE7F6
  BackgroundColor<<historically specific>> #FFEBEE
  BackgroundColor<<personification>> #FFF8E1
}

package "I. Material reproduction\n(transhistorical labour process)" as P1 {
  class Nature <<transhistorical>>
  abstract class LabourProcess <<transhistorical>>
  class UseValue <<transhistorical>>
  class LabourPower <<transhistorical>>
}

package "II. Commodity-value form\n(simple determinations of exchange)" as P2 {
  abstract class AbstractLabour <<abstract det.>> <<social relation>> <<historically specific>>
  abstract class Value <<abstract det.>> <<social relation>> <<historically specific>>
  class Commodity <<historically specific>>
}

package "III. Capitalist production\n(concrete: wage-labour and capital)" as P3 {
  class WageLabourer <<personification>>
  class Capitalist <<personification>>
  class LabourPowerAsCommodity <<historically specific>>
  class Capital <<historically specific>> <<social relation>>
  class CapitalistProductionProcess <<historically specific>>
  class SurplusValue <<historically specific>> <<social relation>>
}

' Force P1 and P2 onto the same rank, with P3 as the sink below both.
' This keeps the two long cross-package hops (P1->P3, P2->P3) running
' downward in parallel instead of arcing through or around P2's box.
P1 -[hidden]right-> P2
P1 -[hidden]down-> P3
P2 -[hidden]down-> P3

Nature --> LabourProcess : materials
LabourPower --> LabourProcess : expended in
LabourProcess --> UseValue : produces

UseValue ..> Commodity : produced for exchange
AbstractLabour --> Value : is the substance of
Commodity *-- UseValue
Commodity *-- Value

LabourPower ..> LabourPowerAsCommodity : sold
LabourProcess ..> CapitalistProductionProcess : + command\n+ valorization
Value ..> Capital : + self-expansion
WageLabourer --> LabourPowerAsCommodity : must sell
Capitalist --> LabourPowerAsCommodity : buys
Capitalist --> Capital : personifies
CapitalistProductionProcess --> SurplusValue : produces
Capital *-- SurplusValue

note as NMethod
  **Method (Grundrisse)**
  Abstract types = simple determinations
  that never exist on their own.
  Concrete types = historically specific
  forms: the concentration of many
  determinations.
  Thought reproduces the concrete;
  it does not generate the real subject
  (society in nature).
end note

@enduml


@startuml surplus-value-labour-process
title I. Material reproduction — the labour process
caption Transhistorical determinations. No surplus value yet: only the metabolism with nature.

hide circle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam ranksep 55
skinparam nodesep 40
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<transhistorical>> #E8F5E9
}

together {
  class Nature <<transhistorical>> {
    independent material world
    laws of matter
  }
  class Human <<transhistorical>> {
    living natural being
    purposive activity
  }
}

class LabourPower <<transhistorical>> {
  mental and physical capacity to work
  embodied in the living individual
}

abstract class LivingLabour <<transhistorical>> {
  expenditure of labour-power
  duration : labour-time
  here only as useful activity
}

abstract class LabourProcess <<transhistorical>> {
  metabolism with nature
  everlasting nature-imposed condition
}

together {
  class ObjectOfLabour <<transhistorical>> {
    material worked upon
  }
  class InstrumentOfLabour <<transhistorical>> {
    placed between worker and object
  }
}

class MeansOfProduction <<transhistorical>> {
  object + instrument
  (from the standpoint of the result)
}

class UseValue <<transhistorical>> {
  useful thing / material wealth
  usefulness is qualitative
}

together {
  class MeansOfSubsistence <<transhistorical>> {
    use-values that reproduce the worker
  }
  class ObjectifiedLabour <<transhistorical>> {
    past living labour fixed in a product
  }
}

Human *-- LabourPower : capacity of
LabourPower --> LivingLabour : expended as
Human --> LabourProcess : conducts
LabourProcess o-- LivingLabour
LabourProcess o-- ObjectOfLabour
LabourProcess o-- InstrumentOfLabour
ObjectOfLabour --|> MeansOfProduction
InstrumentOfLabour --|> MeansOfProduction
Nature --> ObjectOfLabour : earth, materials
LabourProcess --> UseValue : produces
MeansOfSubsistence --|> UseValue
MeansOfProduction --|> UseValue
MeansOfSubsistence ..> LabourPower : reproduces
UseValue --> ObjectifiedLabour : past labour\nin useful form
ObjectifiedLabour ..> InstrumentOfLabour : tools are\npast labour
LivingLabour --> MeansOfProduction : sets in motion

Human -[hidden]down-> LabourProcess
Nature -[hidden]down-> ObjectOfLabour
LabourProcess -[hidden]down-> UseValue
UseValue -[hidden]down-> MeansOfProduction

note right of LabourProcess
  Marx, Capital I, ch. 7:
  examine the labour process first
  independently of any historical form.
  A capitalist at the helm does not
  change its general character:
  humans still transform nature
  to produce use-values.
end note

note bottom of UseValue
  Use-values are the material content of wealth
  in every society. They are not values.
  Petty: labour the father, nature the mother.
end note

@enduml


@startuml surplus-value-commodity
title II. Commodity and value — dual character
caption Simple determinations of commodity production. Value is a social relation, not a physical property.

hide circle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam ranksep 55
skinparam nodesep 45
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<transhistorical>> #E8F5E9
  BackgroundColor<<abstract det.>> #E3F2FD
  BackgroundColor<<social relation>> #EDE7F6
  BackgroundColor<<historically specific>> #FFEBEE
}

abstract class LivingLabour <<transhistorical>> {
  same activity, two social aspects
}

together {
  abstract class ConcreteLabour <<transhistorical>> {
    specific useful activity
    qualitative
  }
  abstract class AbstractLabour <<abstract det.>> <<social relation>> <<historically specific>> {
    homogeneous expenditure of labour-power
    real social abstraction through exchange
  }
}

class UseValue <<transhistorical>> {
  material body of the commodity
}

class Commodity <<historically specific>> {
  article of wealth produced for exchange
}

abstract class Value <<abstract det.>> <<social relation>> <<historically specific>> {
  {abstract}
  magnitude : socially necessary labour-time
}

together {
  class ExchangeValue <<historically specific>> {
    form of appearance of value
    quantitative relation of commodities
  }
  class SociallyNecessaryLabourTime <<social relation>> <<historically specific>> {
    labour-time under average conditions
    of production and skill
  }
}

class Money <<historically specific>> {
  universal equivalent
  independent form of value
  price is value in money
}

class SimpleCommodityProduction <<historically specific>> {
  producer owns means of production
  sells own product
  realises the full value — no surplus value
}

LivingLabour ..|> ConcreteLabour
LivingLabour ..|> AbstractLabour
ConcreteLabour --> UseValue : produces
AbstractLabour --> Value : is the substance of
Commodity *-- UseValue : bearer
Commodity *-- Value : social form
Value --> ExchangeValue : appears as
Value --> SociallyNecessaryLabourTime : magnitude
Money --|> Commodity : one commodity\nas general equivalent
Money --> Value : necessary form\nof appearance
SimpleCommodityProduction --> Commodity : produces\nand sells

LivingLabour -[hidden]down-> Commodity
Commodity -[hidden]down-> Value
Value -[hidden]down-> Money

note right of Value
  Value cannot be found in the
  physical or chemical properties
  of a thing (SPGB; Marx).
  It is a social property that
  appears as a relation between things
  — commodity fetishism.
end note

note bottom of AbstractLabour
  Not a hidden essence inside labour.
  Different concrete labours are actually
  equated when products exchange as values.
  The abstraction is social and historical,
  not metaphysical.
end note

note bottom of SimpleCommodityProduction
  SPGB tailor: owns tools and product,
  sells the coat at its value — no surplus value.
  Surplus value requires wage-labour,
  not commodity exchange as such.
end note

@enduml


@startuml surplus-value-capital
title III. Wage-labour and capital — the production of surplus value
caption Concrete capitalist form. Determinations meet: commodity + class monopoly + labour-power as commodity + unpaid labour.

hide circle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam ranksep 50
skinparam nodesep 35
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<transhistorical>> #E8F5E9
  BackgroundColor<<abstract det.>> #E3F2FD
  BackgroundColor<<social relation>> #EDE7F6
  BackgroundColor<<historically specific>> #FFEBEE
  BackgroundColor<<personification>> #FFF8E1
}

package "Class relation" {
  class CapitalistModeOfProduction <<historically specific>> {
    class monopoly of the means of life
    generalised commodity production
    production for profit
  }
  class WorkingClass <<historically specific>>
  class CapitalistClass <<historically specific>>
  class WageLabourer <<personification>> {
    separated from means of production
    must sell labour-power to live
  }
  class Capitalist <<personification>> {
    personification of capital
    buys LP and MP; appropriates the product
  }
}

package "Labour-power as commodity" {
  class LabourPower <<transhistorical>> {
    capacity to work
  }
  class LabourPowerAsCommodity <<historically specific>> {
    value = SNLT to reproduce the worker
    use-value = living labour (creates value)
  }
  class Wage <<historically specific>> {
    price of labour-power
  }
  class MeansOfSubsistence <<transhistorical>>
  class Commodity <<historically specific>>
}

package "Capital" {
  abstract class Value <<abstract det.>> <<social relation>> <<historically specific>>
  class Capital <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
    value that valorizes itself
    M – C (LP + MP) … P … C' – M'
    c : constant   v : variable   s : surplus
    organicComposition() : c / v
    rateOfSurplusValue() : s / v
    rateOfProfit() : s / (c + v)
  }
  class ConstantCapital <<historically specific>> {
    value in means of production
    transferred, not expanded
  }
  class VariableCapital <<historically specific>> {
    value in labour-power
    the part that expands
  }
  class MeansOfProduction <<transhistorical>>
}

package "Labour process and valorization" {
  abstract class LabourProcess <<transhistorical>>
  class CapitalistProductionProcess <<historically specific>> {
    labour process under command of capital
    product belongs to the capitalist
    extractAbsoluteSurplusValue()
    extractRelativeSurplusValue()
  }
  class ValorizationProcess <<historically specific>> {
    same process counted as labour-time
    beyond the equivalent of v: surplus value
  }
  abstract class LivingLabour <<transhistorical>> {
    creates new value
    transfers value of constant capital
  }
  class WorkingDay <<historically specific>> {
    necessaryLabourTime : reproduces v
    surplusLabourTime : produces s
  }
  class SurplusValue <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
    unpaid labour
    sole source of profit, interest, rent
  }
}

CapitalistModeOfProduction *-- WorkingClass
CapitalistModeOfProduction *-- CapitalistClass
WorkingClass o-- WageLabourer
CapitalistClass o-- Capitalist

WageLabourer --> LabourPowerAsCommodity : sells
Capitalist --> LabourPowerAsCommodity : buys
Capitalist --> MeansOfProduction : monopolizes
WageLabourer ..> MeansOfProduction : excluded from
LabourPowerAsCommodity --|> LabourPower
LabourPowerAsCommodity --|> Commodity
LabourPowerAsCommodity --> Wage : sold at
LabourPowerAsCommodity --> MeansOfSubsistence : value determined by
WageLabourer --> MeansOfSubsistence : consumes

Capital --|> Value
Capital *-- ConstantCapital
Capital *-- VariableCapital
ConstantCapital ..> MeansOfProduction : MP become capital\nonly in this relation
VariableCapital ..> LabourPowerAsCommodity : v laid out as wages
Capitalist --> Capital : advances

CapitalistProductionProcess --|> LabourProcess
CapitalistProductionProcess *-- ValorizationProcess : dual character
CapitalistProductionProcess o-- LivingLabour
CapitalistProductionProcess o-- MeansOfProduction
LabourPowerAsCommodity --> LivingLabour : consumed as
LivingLabour --> ConstantCapital : transfers c
LivingLabour --> VariableCapital : replaces v
LivingLabour --> SurplusValue : creates s
ValorizationProcess --> WorkingDay
WorkingDay --> SurplusValue : surplus labour-time
Capital *-- SurplusValue : appropriates s

note right of LabourPowerAsCommodity
  The worker does not sell labour.
  After the sale, living labour belongs
  to the buyer. Labour-power / labour
  is what makes surplus value compatible
  with exchange of equivalents.
end note

note bottom of SurplusValue
  s/v = rate of exploitation.
  Absolute: lengthen the working day.
  Relative: cheapen means of subsistence
  (raise productivity) and shorten
  necessary labour-time.
  s/(c+v) = rate of profit — a different ratio.
end note

note top of Capital
  Capital is not a thing. Machines are
  means of production; they become capital
  only as a social relation that commands
  wage-labour for surplus value.
end note

@enduml
